From ac09f3209cbe4818f02f3435ce64f0480006da9d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gennro <gennro2004@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 16:06:56 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] arm64ec: generate the FSR2 DX12 shaders with Linux dxc
 instead of SC under wine

gen_fsr2_shaders.sh ran AMD's FidelityFX_SC.exe under wine to get the DXIL
permutation headers, which kept a Windows executable in the build and ruled
out building in a plain Linux (or aarch64) environment. SC is a thin driver
around dxcompiler.dll, so drive Microsoft's DirectXShaderCompiler directly.

gen_fsr2_shaders.py does what SC did: for each of the 8 passes x 4
wave/precision variants it compiles the 64 permutations of the six option
defines with exactly the arguments FSR2's CMake hands SC, shares identical
blobs, and writes headers in SC's layout -- the same PermutationKey bitfield
(first define = bit 0), IndirectionTable and PermutationInfo symbols -- so
ffx_fsr2_shaders_dx12.cpp builds against them unchanged. Reflection comes
from the "Resource Bindings" table of dxc's disassembly. That table is read
from both ends and classified by resource ID, because its type and format
columns run together ("textureunorm_f32") and a whitespace or fixed-width
split silently drops SRVs and UAVs.

Checked against SC 1.6.2112 under wine, with dxc v1.9.2607 built from
source: all 30 headers give the same permutation -> blob grouping (218
unique blobs, as SC), the same reflected CBV/SRV/UAV/sampler names,
registers, counts and spaces in the same order, and for every blob pair
identical disassembly metadata (signatures, cbuffer layouts, bindings,
NumThreads, WaveSize, required features). The code itself differs only as
optimiser versions do (operand order, merged cbuffer loads; IR length
within -1%..+14%). Output is deterministic, and dxc 1.9's built-in
validator signs the container, so libdxil.so is not needed.

build.sh now generates the headers itself when the FSR2 tree is present,
the headers are not, and a dxc is found (DXC=, else PATH), and passes its
LLVM_MINGW down to build_fsr2.sh, which had the x86-64 llvm-mingw path
hard-coded and so could not build with the aarch64-hosted toolchain.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
